Notebook[{Cell["Advanced Lesson 4 - Exercises: Graphics\nMikey Ferguson\nMATH 7013 - Advanced Mathematical Modeling", "Title"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"a", ",", "b", ",", "f", ",", "g", ",", "x", ",", "y", ",", "u", ",", "v", ",", "someData"}], "]"}], ";"}]], "Input"], Cell["1: ParametricPlot with MeshShading, from the Documentation Center", "Section"], Cell["Parts 1 to 3 are a walk through the Documentation Center page for\nParametricPlot. Parts 4 and 5 are below: a MeshShading example copied out of\nthat page, and then the same example with the parameter interval moved from\n0 <= u <= 4 Pi to 2 Pi <= u <= 8 Pi.\nMeshShading takes a list of colours (or None) and paints the successive mesh\nsegments of the curve with them in rotation, so the curve is drawn as an\nalternating dashed ribbon rather than one uniform line.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"ParametricPlot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"u", " ", RowBox[{"Cos", "[", "u", "]"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"u", " ", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "u", "]"}]}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"u", ",", "0", ",", RowBox[{"4", " ", "\[Pi]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"Mesh", "\[Rule]", "20"}], ",", RowBox[{"MeshShading", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"Red", ",", "Blue"}], "}"}]}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"ParametricPlot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"u", " ", RowBox[{"Cos", "[", "u", "]"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"u", " ", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "u", "]"}]}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"u", ",", RowBox[{"2", " ", "\[Pi]"}], ",", RowBox[{"8", " ", "\[Pi]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"Mesh", "\[Rule]", "20"}], ",", RowBox[{"MeshShading", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"Red", ",", "Blue"}], "}"}]}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["Moving the interval does two things at once: the spiral now starts one full\nturn out from the origin rather than at it, and it runs to four turns rather\nthan two, so the whole picture rescales and the inner hole appears.", "Text"], Cell["2: Four trigonometric functions on one graph", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Cos", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Tan", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Cot", "[", "x", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", "0", ",", RowBox[{"2", " ", "\[Pi]"}]}], "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["Tangent and cotangent have poles inside this interval, so the automatic\nvertical range is enormous and sine and cosine are squashed flat against the\naxis. That is the problem Exercise 3 fixes.", "Text"], Cell["3: Restricting the vertical range", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Cos", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Tan", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Cot", "[", "x", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", "0", ",", RowBox[{"2", " ", "\[Pi]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotRange", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"-", "3"}], ",", "3"}], "}"}]}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["4: Giving each curve its own thickness", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Cos", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Tan", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Cot", "[", "x", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", "0", ",", RowBox[{"2", " ", "\[Pi]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotRange", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"-", "3"}], ",", "3"}], "}"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotStyle", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.01`", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.005`", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.003`", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.001`", "]"}]}], "}"}]}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["PlotStyle takes a list matched up with the list of functions, in order:\nsine gets the thickest line, cotangent the thinnest. Thickness is measured\nas a fraction of the overall width of the graphic, not in points, so the\nproportions survive any resizing.", "Text"], Cell["5: Labelling the plot", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Cos", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Tan", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Cot", "[", "x", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", "0", ",", RowBox[{"2", " ", "\[Pi]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotRange", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"-", "3"}], ",", "3"}], "}"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotStyle", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.01`", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.005`", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.003`", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.001`", "]"}]}], "}"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotLabel", "\[Rule]", "\"sin(x), cos(x), tan(x), cot(x)\""}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["6: The label in 12-point Arial Bold", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Cos", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Tan", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Cot", "[", "x", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", "0", ",", RowBox[{"2", " ", "\[Pi]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotRange", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"-", "3"}], ",", "3"}], "}"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotStyle", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.01`", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.005`", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.003`", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.001`", "]"}]}], "}"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotLabel", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"Style", "[", RowBox[{"\"sin(x), cos(x), tan(x), cot(x)\"", ",", RowBox[{"FontFamily", "\[Rule]", "\"Arial\""}], ",", RowBox[{"FontWeight", "\[Rule]", "Bold"}], ",", RowBox[{"FontSize", "\[Rule]", "12"}]}], "]"}]}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["Style wraps any expression in typesetting directives. The three options used\nhere are exactly the three the target picture calls for. If Arial is not\ninstalled the front end substitutes its nearest sans-serif face, which\nchanges the look but not the layout.", "Text"], Cell["7: Adding a frame - the finished graph", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Cos", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Tan", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Cot", "[", "x", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", "0", ",", RowBox[{"2", " ", "\[Pi]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotRange", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"-", "3"}], ",", "3"}], "}"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotStyle", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.01`", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.005`", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.003`", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.001`", "]"}]}], "}"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotLabel", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"Style", "[", RowBox[{"\"sin(x), cos(x), tan(x), cot(x)\"", ",", RowBox[{"FontFamily", "\[Rule]", "\"Arial\""}], ",", RowBox[{"FontWeight", "\[Rule]", "Bold"}], ",", RowBox[{"FontSize", "\[Rule]", "12"}]}], "]"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"Frame", "\[Rule]", "True"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["Frame -> True draws a box with ticks on all four sides and moves the labels\nout to the frame, replacing the default crossed axes. This matches the\nreference picture in the exercise.", "Text"], Cell["8: Plotting data against a fitted curve", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"SeedRandom", "[", "20250825", "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"someData", "=", RowBox[{"Table", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"0.1`", " ", "i"}], ",", RowBox[{SuperscriptBox[RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"0.1`", " ", "i"}], ")"}], "2"], "+", RowBox[{"0.5`", " ", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{RowBox[{"RandomReal", "[", "]"}], "-", "0.5`"}], ")"}]}]}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"i", ",", "1", ",", "20"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}]}]], "Input"], Cell["SeedRandom is not in the professor's version of the exercise. It is here so\nthat the data, the least-squares fit in Exercise 9 and the pictures in\nExercises 10 and 11 all refer to the same twenty points on every re-run of\nthis notebook. Delete that line and the numbers change every evaluation.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"plotData", "=", RowBox[{"ListPlot", "[", RowBox[{"someData", ",", RowBox[{"PlotStyle", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"PointSize", "[", "0.02`", "]"}]}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", "f", "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_", "]"}], "=", SuperscriptBox["x", "2"]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"plotF", "=", RowBox[{"Plot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", "0", ",", "2"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotStyle", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.01`", "]"}]}]}], "]"}]}]}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Show", "[", RowBox[{"plotData", ",", "plotF"}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["PointSize and Thickness are both fractions of the overall graphic width, so\n2% and 1% mean what the exercise says regardless of display size. Show\noverlays finished graphics objects; it does not recompute them, it just\ncomposites them onto one set of axes.", "Text"], Cell["9: A least-squares fit using the basis {1, E^x}", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"g", ",", "x"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"g", "=", RowBox[{"Fit", "[", RowBox[{"someData", ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"1", ",", SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", "x"]}], "}"}], ",", "x"}], "]"}]}]}]], "Input"], Cell["Fit takes the data, a list of basis functions, and the variable they are\nwritten in. It returns the linear combination of those basis functions that\nminimises the sum of squared residuals - here a constant plus a multiple of\nE^x. Nothing about Fit requires the basis to be polynomial; it is linear\nleast squares in the coefficients, not in x.", "Text"], Cell["10: Plotting the fit, dashed", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"plotG", "=", RowBox[{"Plot", "[", RowBox[{"g", ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", "0", ",", "2"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotStyle", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.009`", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Dashing", "[", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"0.02`", ",", "0.02`"}], "}"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["Dashing takes a list of segment lengths, again as fractions of the graphic\nwidth: 2% drawn, 2% blank, repeating. Thickness and Dashing are combined by\nputting both directives in one list.", "Text"], Cell["11: All three on one graph", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Show", "[", RowBox[{"plotData", ",", "plotF", ",", "plotG"}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["The parabola and the exponential fit track each other closely over [0, 2]\nand neither passes through the scatter exactly - which is the honest picture\nof a least-squares fit to noisy data.", "Text"], Cell["12: A parametric rose", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"ParametricPlot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{RowBox[{"Cos", "[", RowBox[{"7", " ", "t"}], "]"}], " ", RowBox[{"Cos", "[", RowBox[{"11", " ", "t"}], "]"}]}], ",", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Cos", "[", RowBox[{"7", " ", "t"}], "]"}], " ", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", RowBox[{"11", " ", "t"}], "]"}]}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"t", ",", "0", ",", RowBox[{"2", " ", "\[Pi]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotStyle", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"Thickness", "[", "0.01`", "]"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"AspectRatio", "\[Rule]", "1"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["AspectRatio -> 1 forces equal vertical and horizontal scaling, without which\nthe figure would be stretched to the default golden-ratio box and the\nrotational symmetry would be destroyed. The variable is named t rather than\ntheta only to keep the input in plain ASCII.", "Text"], Cell["13: Sin[x/Sin[y]] - the same surface eight ways", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot3D", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", FractionBox["x", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "y", "]"}]], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"y", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["Part 1. Trouble is expected wherever Sin[y] = 0 - that is at y = -Pi, y = 0\nand y = Pi. The quotient x/Sin[y] blows up there, so the argument of the\nouter sine sweeps through unboundedly many periods in an arbitrarily small\nneighbourhood of those lines. The function oscillates infinitely fast, and\nno finite sampling grid can represent it. At exactly y = 0 the expression is\nindeterminate.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot3D", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", FractionBox["x", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "y", "]"}]], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"y", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotPoints", "\[Rule]", "20"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot3D", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", FractionBox["x", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "y", "]"}]], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"y", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotPoints", "\[Rule]", "50"}], ",", RowBox[{"Mesh", "\[Rule]", "False"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot3D", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", FractionBox["x", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "y", "]"}]], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"y", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotPoints", "\[Rule]", "200"}], ",", RowBox[{"Mesh", "\[Rule]", "False"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["Parts 2 to 4. Raising PlotPoints resolves more of the structure away from\nthe singular lines and none of it at them - the oscillation near y = 0\nalways outruns the grid. Part 4 is where the cost becomes visible: 200 by\n200 is forty thousand sample points and the evaluation takes noticeably\nlonger for a picture that is no more truthful near the singularities.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"DensityPlot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", FractionBox["x", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "y", "]"}]], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"y", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"Mesh", "\[Rule]", "False"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"DensityPlot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", FractionBox["x", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "y", "]"}]], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"y", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotPoints", "\[Rule]", "20"}], ",", RowBox[{"Mesh", "\[Rule]", "False"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"DensityPlot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", FractionBox["x", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "y", "]"}]], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"y", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotPoints", "\[Rule]", "50"}], ",", RowBox[{"Mesh", "\[Rule]", "False"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"DensityPlot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", FractionBox["x", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "y", "]"}]], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"y", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"PlotPoints", "\[Rule]", "200"}], ",", RowBox[{"Mesh", "\[Rule]", "False"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["Parts 5 to 8. The density plot has the same difficulty for the same reason,\nand it shows it more honestly: near y = 0 and y = +/- Pi the shading\ndegenerates into aliasing artefacts - moire bands that are an artefact of\nthe sampling grid, not features of the function.\nPart 9. Describing the behaviour: away from y = 0 and y = +/- Pi the surface\nis a smooth family of ripples, with the ripples in x getting wider as\n|Sin[y]| grows, because the effective frequency in x is 1/Sin[y]. As y\napproaches any zero of Sin[y] that frequency diverges and the function\noscillates between -1 and +1 infinitely often in any neighbourhood of the\nline. The function stays bounded in [-1, 1] everywhere - it is a sine, after\nall - but it has no limit as y approaches 0, and no continuous extension\nacross those three lines. The correct conclusion from these eight pictures\nis that no amount of resolution will ever render this function faithfully\nnear y = 0, and the way to know that is analysis, not a bigger PlotPoints.", "Text"], Cell["14: Manipulate - dynamic 2-D and 3-D plots", "Section"], Cell["The three cells below are Manipulate expressions. They build correctly under\na plain kernel but the sliders are only live inside a Wolfram front end\n(Mathematica or Wolfram Player). Evaluated through Wolfram Engine under\nJupyter they render as a static snapshot at the initial parameter values.", "Text"], Cell["Manipulate[Plot[E^(-a x), {x, 0, 1}], {a, 0, 10}]", "Input"], Cell["Part 2. The plot jumps as the slider moves because the automatic vertical\nrange is recomputed for each value of a. Fixing PlotRange to All - or to an\nexplicit interval - pins the axes so the curve moves against a fixed frame.", "Text"], Cell["Manipulate[Plot[E^(-a x), {x, 0, 1}, PlotRange -> All], {a, 0, 10}]", "Input"], Cell["Manipulate[\n Plot3D[E^(-a x - b y), {x, 0, 1}, {y, 0, 1}, PlotRange -> All],\n {a, 0, 10}, {b, 0, 10, 2}]", "Input"], Cell["Part 3. A control specified as {a, 0, 10} is continuous; adding a step, as\nin {b, 0, 10, 2}, makes it discrete - the slider snaps to 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10\nand nothing between.", "Text"], Cell["15: A dynamic algebraic calculator", "Section"], Cell["Manipulate[Expand[(x + y)^n], {n, 0, 10, 1}]", "Input"], Cell["Same discrete-control mechanism as above: the step of 1 restricts n to\nintegers, which is the only thing Expand can usefully do with an exponent\nhere.", "Text"], Cell["16: Analysing 2x^2 + 3x^3 y == 4x^4 + 5y + 6", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"f", ",", "x", ",", "y"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"sol16", "=", RowBox[{"Solve", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{RowBox[{RowBox[{"2", " ", SuperscriptBox["x", "2"]}], "+", RowBox[{"3", " ", SuperscriptBox["x", "3"], " ", "y"}]}], "\[Equal]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"4", " ", SuperscriptBox["x", "4"]}], "+", RowBox[{"5", " ", "y"}], "+", "6"}]}], ",", "y"}], "]"}]}]}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_", "]"}], "=", RowBox[{"y", "/.", "\[VeryThinSpace]", RowBox[{"First", "[", "sol16", "]"}]}]}]], "Input"], Cell["Parts 1 and 2. The equation is linear in y, so there is a single solution,\nand extracting the right-hand side of that one rule gives an explicit\nrational function of x. Immediate assignment is right here: the expression\nis fixed and needs no re-evaluation per call.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "4"}], ",", "4"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["Part 3. The plot has a vertical asymptote where the denominator 3x^3 - 5\nvanishes, near x = 1.19.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Simplify", "[", RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["f", "\[Prime]", MultilineFunction -> None], "[", "x", "]"}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["f", "\[Prime]", MultilineFunction -> None], "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "4"}], ",", "4"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["Parts 4 and 5. The derivative is another rational function, with a double\npole at the same place.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Solve", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["f", "\[Prime]", MultilineFunction -> None], "[", "x", "]"}], "\[Equal]", "0"}], ",", "x"}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["Part 6. Setting the derivative to zero clears the denominator and leaves a\nsixth-degree polynomial. Degree six has no solution by radicals in general,\nso Solve returns Root objects: exact algebraic numbers specified by the\npolynomial they satisfy and an index saying which root is meant. They are\nexact and perfectly usable, but nobody can read them.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"NSolve", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["f", "\[Prime]", MultilineFunction -> None], "[", "x", "]"}], "\[Equal]", "0"}], ",", "x"}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["Part 7. NSolve returns the same six roots numerically: two complex conjugate\npairs' worth of complex values and four real ones.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"roots16", "=", RowBox[{"Chop", "[", RowBox[{"x", "/.", "\[VeryThinSpace]", RowBox[{"NSolve", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["f", "\[Prime]", MultilineFunction -> None], "[", "x", "]"}], "\[Equal]", "0"}], ",", "x"}], "]"}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"realRoots16", "=", RowBox[{"Select", "[", RowBox[{"roots16", ",", RowBox[{RowBox[{RowBox[{"Im", "[", "#1", "]"}], "\[Equal]", "0"}], "&"}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"TableForm", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Table", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"r", ",", RowBox[{"Chop", "[", RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["f", "\[Prime]", MultilineFunction -> None], "[", "r", "]"}], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"Chop", "[", RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["f", "\[Prime]\[Prime]", MultilineFunction -> None], "[", "r", "]"}], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"If", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["f", "\[Prime]\[Prime]", MultilineFunction -> None], "[", "r", "]"}], "<", "0"}], ",", "\"local maximum\"", ",", "\"local minimum\""}], "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"r", ",", "realRoots16"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"TableHeadings", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"None", ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"\"x\"", ",", "\"f'[x]\"", ",", "\"f''[x]\"", ",", "\"classification\""}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["Part 8. The six roots split into two complex ones and four real ones, and\nthe four real ones do agree with the picture in Part 5 - each is a place\nwhere the derivative curve crosses zero. Classifying them by the sign of the\nsecond derivative gives, to four figures: x = -0.8427 and x = 0.2671 are\nlocal maxima of f, while x = 0 and x = 1.9615 are local minima. So the two\nreal roots corresponding to local maxima are -0.8427 and 0.2671.\nOne caution when reading the derivative plot. The sign of f' also flips\nacross the vertical asymptote near x = 1.19, and that flip is not a critical\npoint - the derivative is undefined there, not zero, which is exactly why\nNSolve does not return it.", "Text"]}, WindowSize -> {1100, 850}, StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"]
(* End of Notebook Content *)
